{
  "beruf": "pflege",
  "title": "Care work: older people at home and in residences",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "spanish-for-work run, 2026-11-07: written by hand for Spain, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon or in another deck are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Care for older people is one of the most common first jobs for newcomers in Spain, in a residencia or in someone's home. This deck has the people and places, daily care, health and safety, the routine of a shift, and how to talk to families and colleagues. Most care jobs call older people usted, so the phrases do too.",
  "introEasy": "Do you care for older people in Spain? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Cuidador/a de personas mayores",
    "Auxiliar de enfermería",
    "Gerocultor/a",
    "Auxiliar de ayuda a domicilio",
    "Cuidador/a interno/a"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Say who you are and what you are going to do before you touch someone: «Buenos días, señora Ruiz. Soy Amina. Voy a ayudarla a levantarse.» Then wait for the answer.",
    "Use usted and the person's name: don Manuel, doña Carmen. Your colleagues will mostly say tú to you, and to each other.",
    "Never guess with medication. If you are not sure, ask: «¿Me lo puede confirmar la enfermera?» Write down what happened and tell the next shift.",
    "Paid care work can need a recognised qualification, and the rules differ by region and employer. Ask the employer, the regional social services or a union. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
